  "notes": "Modified cash basis; governmental fund balance treated as cash (p6: beginning 833,892, ending 918,964). Governmental lines from Total Governmental Funds columns (pp5-6). Rural fire reimbursement 30,590 is printed under Other Financing Sources so recorded in other_financing. Business-type from proprietary statement p8 total column (net position measure); receipts total computed (no single printed total); business-type transfers in 31,324 (from general/debt service) in business_type.transfers_net. Business-type reconcile: 3,162,230 + 276,852 - 325,238 + 31,324 = 3,145,168. Debt payments interest 11,642 only for water (no principal shown since cash basis net position basis). Statement of Net Position (p1) Total column used for balance sheet. Detail: only an all-funds budget schedule (p9, budgetary basis) is in the trimmed PDF; no per-fund budget schedules, so Water/Sewer are not separable; lines that mix governmental and business-type are marked review and listed as governmental, Public Works treated as business-type. Transfers in/out lines are in section other financing and included in the printed totals. p10 is a fund-balance reconciliation (beginning 1,114,452, ending 1,278,018), not itemized. Read pages 1-10 of 10.",
  "decision_needed": [
    {
      "question": "The p9 Budget and Actual - All Funds schedule prints Charges for services 341,671, State grants 132,484, Investment earnings 16,571, Capital outlay 57,838 and Debt service 197,229 as single lines that mix governmental and Water/Sewer amounts (other statements suggest e.g. 75,003 gov + 266,668 water/sewer charges; debt service = 80,000 principal + 97,688 interest gov + 19,541 water). p9 gives no breakdown, so they are not split. Keep them as single governmental lines, or label them otherwise?",
      "amount": 745793,
      "recorded_as": "detail lines 3, 4, 8, 19, 20 kept as printed, fund_type governmental with review; Debt service as debt service principal/debt principal (combined P&I rule)",
      "source_page": 9,
      "computed": "sum of the five mixed lines"
    }
  ]
}
